Part 1 — Basic HTTP GET/Response

Look for packets like:

GET /wireshark-labs/HTTP-wireshark-file1.html HTTP/1.1

and:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK

Click packets and inspect details.

Answers You Need to Find
a) Browser HTTP version & Server HTTP version
In GET packet:

Expand:

Hypertext Transfer Protocol

You’ll see:

GET ... HTTP/1.1

Meaning:

Browser uses HTTP/1.1
In Response packet:

You’ll see:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK

Meaning:

Server also uses HTTP/1.1
b) Languages accepted by browser

In the GET request headers, find:

Accept-Language:

Example:

Accept-Language: en-US,en;q=0.9

This means browser accepts:

English (US)
English
c) Status code returned

In the response packet:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK

Status code:

200 OK
d) Last modified date

In response headers, find:

Last-Modified:

Example:

Last-Modified: Sat, 10 Dec 2022 12:30:00 GMT

That is the answer.

e) Number of bytes returned

Find:

Content-Length:

Example:

Content-Length: 128

Means:

128 bytes returned.
Part 2 — HTTP Conditional GET

Now repeat capture for:

http://gaia.cs.umass.edu/wireshark-labs/HTTP-wireshark-file2.html
Procedure
First Request
Start capture
Open URL first time
Stop capture
Second Request (Important)
Start capture again
Refresh the page in browser
Stop capture

Now analyze both GET requests.

Questions & Answers
a) First GET — Is IF-MODIFIED-SINCE present?

Open first GET packet.

Look for:

If-Modified-Since:

Usually:

NO, it is not present in the first request.

Reason:
Browser has not cached the file yet.

b) Did server explicitly return file contents?

Check server response.

If you see:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK

AND:

Content-Length:

AND HTML data below,

then:

YES, server returned the file contents.
c) Second GET — Is IF-MODIFIED-SINCE present?

Open second GET packet.

Now you should see:

If-Modified-Since: <date>

Example:

If-Modified-Since: Sat, 10 Dec 2022 12:30:00 GMT

This date comes from the earlier Last-Modified header.

Status Code for Second GET

Server usually replies:

HTTP/1.1 304 Not Modified

Meaning:

File has NOT changed
Browser should use cached copy
Did server return file contents in second response?

NO.

How to tell:

No HTML content
Very small packet size
No Content-Length
Status code = 304 Not Modified
Easy Summary Table
Question	Expected Answer
Browser HTTP Version	HTTP/1.1
Server HTTP Version	HTTP/1.1
Accepted Language	From Accept-Language header
Status Code	200 OK
Last Modified	From Last-Modified header
Bytes Returned	From Content-Length
First GET has IF-MODIFIED-SINCE?	No
Second GET has IF-MODIFIED-SINCE?	Yes
Second Response Code	304 Not Modified
File returned again?	No
Useful Wireshark Filters
Purpose	Filter
Only HTTP	http
GET Requests	http.request.method == "GET"
Responses	http.response
Status 200	http.response.code == 200
Status 304	http.response.code == 304
